    ("In the Puttaswamy-I (Privacy, 2017) judgment, what is the three-fold proportionality test established for any state interference with privacy?", "Legality (backed by valid law), Legitimate State Aim, and Proportionality (least intrusive means with adequate safeguards)", ["Executive convenience, political popularity, and low administrative cost", "Cabinet approval, police recommendation, and international treaty compliance", "Public referendum, judicial consensus, and presidential proclamation"], "Any privacy infringement must pass the three-pronged standard: statutory authority, legitimate purpose, and proportional necessity."),
    ("In 'K.S. Puttaswamy (Aadhaar, 2018)' judgment, which Section of the Aadhaar Act, 2016 was struck down as unconstitutional?", "Section 57 (insofar as it permitted private corporations to mandate Aadhaar authentication for commercial contracts)", ["Section 3 (issuance of Aadhaar numbers to residents)", "Section 7 (mandating Aadhaar for subsidies and welfare benefits)", "Section 28 (security and confidentiality of biometric data)"], "The Constitution Bench held that commercial exploitation of Aadhaar by private telecom and banks violated individual privacy."),
    ("In the 'Shreya Singhal v. Union of India' (2015) judgment, which Section of the Information Technology Act was struck down completely?", "Section 66A (criminalizing sending offensive messages via computers, held vague and violative of Article 19(1)(a))", ["Section 69A (blocking websites in national security interest)", "Section 79 (intermediary safe harbor provisions)", "Section 43A (compensation for failure to protect data)"], "The Supreme Court struck down Section 66A for chilling free speech and suffering from the vice of unconstitutional overbreadth."),
